What we call a WOW experience. And the marketing starts, you know, it could be the website, it could be social media, it could be they know somebody. It really starts when they first call our office and when they look at our website. So we have a… website, it's pretty much a masterclass in building a site. We have over 1,400 pages. It's all my own material, and every week we're building it. So we're constantly teaching, constantly educating. And it starts with our core values, which are educating. So we always educate our patients.

01:24:22.000 --> 01:24:23.000
Hmm.

01:24:23.000 --> 01:24:34.000
I always commit to them that I'm there for them for the rest of their lives so that we can get them healthy and keep them healthy. And we're very serious in what we do. It's not about selling the case, it's about getting them healthy. And we tell every patient at their first visit, we're going to do 3 things for them. We're going to get them healthier. We're going to improve their aesthetics, we're going to improve function.

01:24:34.000 --> 01:24:44.000
We also tell them if they're in the right place because probably about 8 or 10% of our patients have severe dental phobia, and almost nobody likes being there. Very few people like to come in.

01:24:44.000 --> 01:24:55.000
Well, in our practice, people say, we like coming here. It's just like a spa, but relax because we're very nice to them. So there's a lot of different touch points of marketing. There are probably about 20 before I even meet the patient.

01:24:55.000 --> 01:25:12.000
And I don't think social media is the most important, because there's a lot of noise out there, and everybody is clamoring, you know, for those patients. As I'm building my teaching business more, and I'm doing more educating, you know, everybody has their own teaching center. Like, I've been teaching implants for 35 years, and I was one of the first to have a teaching center.

01:25:12.000 --> 01:25:21.000
Now, I don't do as much in my practice, but there's 250 implant teaching centers in the United States, so everybody's the expert, so everybody's the best dentist in town.

01:25:21.000 --> 01:25:39.000
And I don't think you need a tremendous number of patients, you just need a number of patients that really respect and care about what you're doing. You know, you don't want to compete on price. You really don't want to compete at all. I don't look at other dentists as my competition. I don't look at anybody as my competition. If I give really good value.

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:53.000
It seems to work. And but it won't be overnight. You can't really make it overnight success. Not a strong one. You can be successful quickly, but you really won't have the same type of death. You won't have roots, you know?

01:25:53.000 --> 01:26:04.000
I really like the analogies in that especially with regards to, you know, you want to be the person that patients are thinking of when they do break a tooth.

01:26:04.000 --> 01:26:05.000
And not necessarily when they're meeting.

01:26:05.000 --> 01:26:13.000
Yeah. Nobody. Nobody has no one would ever say to you, Don, you know, go to my dentist. Why is he goes cheap.

01:26:13.000 --> 01:26:15.000
It's really cheap dentist.

01:26:15.000 --> 01:26:26.000
That's right. That's right. That's a whole, uh, mindset shift from a PPO practice to one that is focused on high-quality care of treatment and patients.

01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:34.000
It's giving people value. You know, one of my mentors says, you have to care more about the value that you give than the money you receive.

01:26:34.000 --> 01:26:44.000
And I don't ever not do treatment because of money. I always find a way to make it go within their budget. Now I won't lower my quality. I might do it over a longer period of time.

01:26:44.000 --> 01:26:56.000
Hmm.

01:26:56.000 --> 01:26:57.000
Yeah.

01:26:57.000 --> 01:27:04.000
We have something that we do in our practice. It's, I mean, every practice should do it. Did you know that 90% of all practices don't do a comprehensive examination on their patients? That's pretty easy, you know? My daughter, who's a dentist, just bought a practice, and she's doing, and they didn't do comprehensive examinations.

01:27:04.000 --> 01:27:22.000
And within five weeks. She's swamped, and the practice wasn't that busy, but she's there just doing comprehensive exam, and they didn't want to pay for it, because 90% of her patients take insurance. So she bought a practice that's insurance-based in a great community in a small.

01:27:22.000 --> 01:27:38.000
It's boutique practice that she's creating within 5 weeks, she's given away free exams, free X-rays to everybody. And guess what? The return on that investment is tremendous. And now they they new equipment. I've watched it. Of course, she's my daughter, so I've trained her in this, you know, techniques.

01:27:38.000 --> 01:27:41.000
And I've watched it work very quickly. So yeah.

01:27:41.000 --> 01:27:47.000
Amazing. I hope the practice owners listening in can take that and try it out. Something to really consider.

01:27:47.000 --> 01:27:57.000
Okay. Yeah, I was about to say 90% of all practices don't do a comprehensive exam, don't take x-rays, don't do periodontal probing.

01:27:57.000 --> 01:28:06.000
There's a lot of work out there. And the United States, you know, 90% of the periodontal treatment, I mean, the periodontal disease is not being diagnosed. Only about 3% of the people get treatment. It's an epidemic.

01:28:06.000 --> 01:28:10.000
Yeah.

01:28:10.000 --> 01:28:25.000
Absolutely. Now, Danielle, the moment a caller asks, do you take my insurance? What needs to happen in the next 90 seconds to turn that call into a booked appointment?

01:28:25.000 --> 01:28:42.000
Well, first, let's talk about what is happening, because I think it's important to talk about the stuff that is happening that's deterring patients, or it's getting the patients into the practices that the doctors then say, I don't want these patients here.

01:28:42.000 --> 01:29:01.000
But our front office team members are essentially letting them in because they're transactional, and that's the only way to describe those phone calls is the patient is calling with an initial concern, and they've put insurance. Do you accept my insurance plan?

01:29:01.000 --> 01:29:20.000
On top of that initial concern, because they know that something could cost money, and they don't know how much. So they want to understand right away when they call, is this going to be taken care of? Now, I personally feel like insurance is and I explain it to a lot of my clients that insurance is sort of like a discount plan.

01:29:20.000 --> 01:29:42.000
We're just going to get $1,000 a year, and then the rest is going to come out of pocket, so let's learn to love it. And that's how I throw it out into the world. I'm very nonchalant about it, and most of my clients are like, all right, you're spot on. That's really what it is. And I think that in the dental industry, we've gone the extreme opposite of trying to teach.

01:29:42.000 --> 01:29:48.000
everyone their insurance versus teaching our team members not to be transactional.

01:29:48.000 --> 01:30:18.000
So within that first 90 seconds, we have really got to earn the trust of the patient. We're going to acknowledge the fact that that was a major concern. That's the first thing they asked us. So we're certainly going to answer the question, do we accept your plan? We accept all major PPO plans.

01:30:22.000 --> 01:30:23.000
Hmm.

01:30:23.000 --> 01:30:29.000
That's the truth. Are we in network with all of them? No, we're going to be a non-preferred provider, but we can accept that, and we're going to bill for you, and this is how it's going to work. Now we're going to redirect the phone call. We want to understand what the value is that they're looking for. Why are they calling us? Were they referred? Are they in pain?

01:30:29.000 --> 01:30:46.000
What can I do to support what you're calling me for? How can I help you? And I think once we start to build that trust, start to edify our team, teach this caller how wonderful our providers are and our team members, now we've sort of made insurance.

01:30:46.000 --> 01:30:47.000
No.

01:30:47.000 --> 01:31:07.000
Secondary. They've asked us the question, we've answered it, but we've made the insurance question really secondary to everything else that we've just taught them about our practice. But that 90 seconds is… it's… It's everything to that initial call into the practice, and we've got to really build our team members up to understand how to handle that.

01:31:07.000 --> 01:31:16.000
Right? So what you're saying is basically don't be giving out answers, but rather focus on building trust.

01:31:16.000 --> 01:31:31.000
I think we give the answer right? We do have to be transparent about how we accept insurance in our space. But we also need to be building that relationship from the start with our practice. If we're not doing that, then what are we doing?

01:31:31.000 --> 01:32:01.000
And again, I go back to I listen in a lot of phone calls and I travel into a lot of offices weekly. And those conversations are so transactional. And then the flip side is… How did I get here with these patients? How? Why don't they understand what our philosophy is and what our culture is that we're trying to build? And when we go back and sort of replay with that initial, you know, encounter was, it's right there. It was right there on that initial phone call, right where we had the opportunity to build that relationship with the patient.

01:32:05.000 --> 01:32:20.000
So it's on that initial 90 seconds when that phone call happens and that one question that we all hate in dentistry happens, but we can divert and we can start to redirect and we can teach value right from that question.

01:32:20.000 --> 01:32:35.000
Thank you very much, Daniel, for that answer. Dr. Sonic, in the 1st 90 days after a staff training, what's the one behavior you watch for that tells you whether patients are actually going to start saying yes more?

01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:41.000
Well, it's not like we hire somebody. upon the patients.

01:32:41.000 --> 01:32:48.000
Because we already have the culture. So when we hire somebody, I think what you're asking is, how do you know when you hire somebody, they get it?

01:32:48.000 --> 01:32:49.000
That's fine.

01:32:49.000 --> 01:33:05.000
You know, because my team has it. I have 25 people in our practice, and I'd hire all of them again. And that's rare. And we've had people there, you know, some of them have been there a couple of years, some of them in there 25 years. It's all over the place. So we have a very strong culture of people who are, and Danielle said it, you know, we talked about it all the time.

01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:21.000
We don't want transactional relationships. We want transformative relationships. And then she says 90 seconds. I use that rule all the time. It's really not even 90 seconds, it's like 10 seconds. It's like that. The phone is very hard, though, to make that… to have a relationship, because you don't… you don't get to see.

01:33:21.000 --> 01:33:41.000
what they look like and how they're responding. Um, so how do I know if they get it? Well, it all starts in the hiring process. And that's a, that's a whole, that's a whole different topic, but I'll quickly give it to you because it's a big problem that people have. They, they hire anybody to fill spots because we don't have many people that want to work right now. People that want to go to work, they want to work from home.

01:33:41.000 --> 01:33:56.000
COVID sort of changed that a lot. So I don't care what business that you're in in the country, and I'm all over the country, people say, I can't find people. Well, I can find them, but I don't have, like, 50 people a week that I would hire. I go through.

01:33:56.000 --> 01:34:13.000
This is going to sound crazy. 300 to 400 people before I hire anybody. So it's very hard to get a job in our practice. And we need people. We need 3 people now, and my partner called me up today, says someone's going to 3 days a week for 4, because I want you to hire this person.

01:34:13.000 --> 01:34:27.000
And I didn't like her. I didn't think she'd fit. You know, I knew… I knew within 10 seconds of meeting her, she made me very anxious, and she made me anxious to make her a patient anxious. I didn't want to hire her. And he told me to hire somebody else that was one of his patients.

01:34:27.000 --> 01:34:41.000
And I didn't like her either. I didn't think she was good. And we hired her and we tried to make her fit our culture. It doesn't work that way. You can't change people. And she was with us for, like, 5 months, and she pretty much.

01:34:41.000 --> 01:35:01.000
brought the karma down in the office. We're working so hard to work with her, and she just, you know, she quit. I very rarely fire anybody. People usually just leave, but I never really have people quit, because by the time I hire somebody, they're the right fit. How do we figure out what the right fit client is? Well, you have to understand your culture, and you have to know what you're doing. You have to be very clear, and like you would say, conscious. I'm very conscious of my culture.

01:35:01.000 --> 01:35:18.000
We have five core values. You know, we're team-hearted, you know, we're good servants, we're good to each other, okay? We're educational, we're health-oriented. Okay, we're really all work very, very hard together, and everyone knows what our core values are. They all know what our mission statement is, to improve the quality of patients' lives.

01:35:18.000 --> 01:35:30.000
To give everybody a WOW experience. We don't have a dental practice. We have a spa. We happen to do dentistry in our spa, okay? It's a healthcare practice that we improve lives. That's where everybody is. So how do I know if they're going to work out?

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:51.000
They're gone… I don't even hire them. My team does. We do we do a resume, we do a culture index, which is which is something that tells us what their personality is. We do a FaceTime interview. They come in for a blink interview. If that works out, they spend a half day, then it's been the full day. We have so many touch points. And then at the end, my staff is the one that says.

01:35:51.000 --> 01:35:52.000
Mm-hmm.

01:35:52.000 --> 01:36:05.000
Yeah, I like this person and I'm fighting with my partner. I'm not fighting with him because we're going to be really short staffed this summer. I just don't want to bring in this person. And I might just bring her in just to prove that don't, don't bring in the wrong people because you really want to hire slow and fire fast, as they say.

01:36:05.000 --> 01:36:23.000
And when someone comes in, we know immediately, I know immediately if they're going to work at our practice. And people who are going to want to work here want to work at our practice, because they've read our website, they see what we do, they understand what we do. We have almost a thousand Google reviews. Now, we've written 7 books, you know, I mean, we're like a well-known practice in the area.

01:36:23.000 --> 01:36:33.000
But I wasn't wasn't 40 years ago. My first year in practice, I grossed $35,000 with 90,000 overhead and forged thousand debt in 1985.

01:36:33.000 --> 01:36:42.000
You know, went bankrupt a few years after that, you know, blew it all up. People go, you're so successful. How come you're so successful? I said, because I've made every mistake in the book.

01:36:42.000 --> 01:36:58.000
You know, and it really comes down to four words. Be nice to people. Forget your marketing strategy. Just be really nice for people. Be honest. That's your niche. Be comprehensive. Treat the patients that it's the golden rule. It's so basic.

01:36:58.000 --> 01:37:14.000
It's so easy. It's like, you know, how many books are there in a diet? And in the bookstore? It's like thousands of books on how to lose weight. You know how you lose weight? Stop eating. You're going to lose weight. You want to build business practice. Be really nice to your patients and have good clinical skills.

01:37:14.000 --> 01:37:24.000
And study hard. But nobody wants to do that because it's boring. There's really no I don't know anybody that became successful overnight. It's really, really hard, you know? And the ones that did like Britney Spears and Michael Jackson, they had hard lives, you know?

01:37:24.000 --> 01:37:28.000
Right. Absolutely.

01:37:28.000 --> 01:37:34.000
But it's… I don't see too many dentists becoming great. I mean, only on Instagram, not in real life.

01:37:34.000 --> 01:37:35.000
So now I'm ranting.

01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:52.000
That's right. So absolutely appreciate that answer, Dr. Sonic, where you showed, you know, if you're having a culture problem, that's really a hiring problem, and to answer, uh, you know, if patients are actually going to start saying yes more.

01:37:52.000 --> 01:38:02.000
Well, you'll have to start at that process and be as adamant as Dr. Sonic is with hiring people slow.

