01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:39.000
Dr. Michael Tooman. Oh, are asking about the how. So Danielle, when a practice decides to go out of network, what is the best way to notify the patients? Is it a mass email before the next recall, or is it better to have that conversation face to face?

01:43:39.000 --> 01:43:57.000
Yeah, I'm a firm believer that this conversation is at the appointment, knee to knee with your patients and really sort of talking them through this process. I have gone out of network with that big bad Delta a few times, and.

01:43:57.000 --> 01:44:10.000
It's been in every area, so I've really found that the patients respond well when we're, again, transparent. You've built trust, they understand why they're staying with your practice.

01:44:10.000 --> 01:44:28.000
we didn't see this huge 30% drop in patient base. What we did see was that maybe they trickled away for a little bit of time, and they came right back because they loved the team. They loved the doctor, they loved the way that they were treated.

01:44:28.000 --> 01:44:47.000
But certainly, I don't think if we would have sent out a mass text or an email or, you know, anything like that, a letter that they would have felt the same way. I think they would have felt a little blindsided if we didn't have that conversation with them, their favorite hygienist, their assistants, the doctor.

01:44:47.000 --> 01:44:55.000
really talking them through with this process was going to look like. And in most cases, I started this process a year in advance.

